# SilexGIS deployment configuration — copy to .env and edit.

# REQUIRED: database password (internal network only, but pick a strong one).
SILEXGIS_DB_PASSWORD=change-me

# Public URL users reach the app on. Must match exactly (OIDC redirect URIs derive from it).
# Behind a TLS proxy, use the https:// address.
SILEXGIS_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Host port the web service listens on (plain HTTP; ignored when using the TLS overlay).
SILEXGIS_HTTP_PORT=8080

# --- TLS (only when using docker-compose.tls.yml, which adds Caddy + Let's Encrypt) --------
# Both are REQUIRED with that overlay (unused otherwise).
# Public hostname whose DNS points at this host; ports 80 and 443 must be reachable.
# SILEXGIS_DOMAIN=caves.example.org
# Contact address Let's Encrypt uses for expiry/revocation notices.
# SILEXGIS_TLS_EMAIL=admin@example.org
# Remember to set SILEXGIS_PUBLIC_URL=https://caves.example.org above.

# --- Database browser (only when using docker-compose.pgadmin.yml, which adds pgAdmin) ----
# The password is REQUIRED with that overlay (unused otherwise): pgAdmin stores database
# credentials, so it gets its own login rather than a shipped default.
# SILEXGIS_PGADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
# SILEXGIS_PGADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# Loopback-only host port for the pgAdmin web UI.
# SILEXGIS_PGADMIN_PORT=5050
#
# Development only: docker-compose.dbport.yml publishes the database itself on this
# loopback port for desktop clients. Never add that overlay to a deployed installation.
# SILEXGIS_DB_HOST_PORT=5433

# --- Mobile-client development server (docker-compose.speleoloc-dev.yml) ------------------
# Development only, and a standalone project rather than an overlay: it never composes with
# the stack above, and it hard-codes development passwords. Both host ports below are bound
# to 127.0.0.1 and both are claimed by that file, so nothing else here may take them.
# 5434 rather than 5432 (docker-compose.dev.yml) or 5433 (the overlay just above).
# SILEXGIS_SPELEOLOC_DB_PORT=5434
# SILEXGIS_SPELEOLOC_API_PORT=5205

# --- Notifications ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How often queued notifications are sent. 0 switches sending off but keeps queuing them.
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_POLL_SECONDS=15
# Hour (UTC) at which daily summaries go out, for users who chose one message a day.
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_DIGEST_HOUR_UTC=7
# How long a notification stays readable in the recipient's list before it is deleted. Read
# and unread alike, and whether or not an email ever went out for it. Zero or less is
# ignored in favour of the default, so a mistyped setting cannot empty somebody's list.
# The one notification setting an administrator can also change from the admin pages, under
# Messaging. Saving it there replaces this value; leave the page alone and this stays in force.
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# How the header's unread count learns it has changed. 'poll' asks again on a timer and is
# the only transport implemented; 'sse' is reserved for a server-pushed stream.
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_BADGE_TRANSPORT=poll
# The hours in which nothing leaves the installation, as wall-clock times in each recipient's
# own day. Both ends are required: with only one set there is no window and mail goes out as
# it always has. A message may cross midnight — 22:00 to 07:00 is one window, not two. What
# is held back is only what leaves; the notification itself is readable at once, and the
# categories nobody may switch off ignore the window because a warning that waits until
# morning is not a warning.
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_QUIET_HOURS_FROM=22:00
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_QUIET_HOURS_TO=07:00
# Whose night to use for somebody whose browser has never reported a zone. An IANA zone name;
# for a club, the one nearly all of its members are in.
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_TIME_ZONE=Europe/Bucharest

# --- Terrain (optional; only when using docker-compose.terrain.yml) -------------------------
# Real relief under the caves in the 3D view. With none of this set the globe is the smooth
# reference ellipsoid, which needs nothing installed, downloaded or pre-baked.
#
# Bake a pyramid first — `node deploy/terrain.mjs fetch`, then `... bake` — and it prints the
# exact lines below for what it produced. See "Terrain" in docs/INSTALL.md.
#
# Host directory holding layer.json. REQUIRED with that overlay (unused otherwise).
# SILEXGIS_TERRAIN_DIR=/srv/silexgis/terrain
# Where a browser asks for the tiles. Must match the mount above; the trailing slash matters.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__Url=/terrain/
# What the tile heights are measured from: Orthometric (above sea level, which is what an
# unconverted elevation model holds) or Ellipsoidal (converted when baked). Wrong here puts
# every cave about forty metres off its hillside with nothing on screen to say so.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__HeightDatum=Orthometric
# The local geoid undulation in metres, used ONLY with Ellipsoidal. Measured EGM2008 values over
# Romanian karst run +39 to +45 m.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__GeoidHeightM=0
# Credit the elevation data's licence requires; shown on the scene. It belongs to whatever you
# actually baked, so take the line the bake printed rather than the example below — which is the
# Copernicus GLO-30 credit and is true only of a pyramid baked from Copernicus data.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__Attribution=Copernicus DEM GLO-30 — © DLR e.V. 2010-2014 and © Airbus Defence and Space GmbH 2014-2018 provided under COPERNICUS by the European Union and ESA
# Where a build started from inside the application does its work. Each build gets a folder of its
# own here holding the rasters it was given and what it makes of them; they are kept until somebody
# deletes the build, so this wants a disk chosen for size. Nothing is written here until somebody
# starts a build.
#
# The compose stack already sets this to /data/terrain/builds, which is a named volume, so leave it
# alone unless that disk is too small. A path named here is a path INSIDE the api container and must
# be mounted into it — pointing this at a host directory without also mounting it puts tens of
# gigabytes in the container's own writable layer, where they disappear on the next recreate. To use
# a bigger disk, bind-mount it over /data/terrain in docker-compose.yml rather than changing this —
# the compose file's own mapping wins over anything set here in any case.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__BuildRoot=/data/terrain/builds
# Where a finished, checked pyramid is MOVED so the web server can serve it, at
# /terrain/builds/<build id>/. A directory of its own holding nothing but finished tiles, and the
# only directory a serving rule may ever be pointed at: one level up are the rasters the builds
# were made from, and there is no sign-in in front of any of it. Much smaller than the build root.
# The compose stack sets this and the shipped web configuration is built around it, so changing it
# means changing that serving rule to match and rebuilding the web image.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__PublishRoot=/data/terrain/published
# How long one cell of elevation may take to arrive before the attempt is abandoned. A cell is some
# tens of megabytes, so twenty minutes is generous on any usable link and still short enough that a
# connection which has stopped delivering does not hold a build open until somebody notices.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__CellTimeoutSeconds=1200

# --- Terrain baking (optional; only when using docker-compose.terrain-worker.yml) -------------
# Lets the application bake a pyramid itself, from an area chosen inside it, instead of somebody
# running the bake by hand. The tile-making step runs as a service of its own, because it is a
# 1.4 GB image and several gigabytes of memory that an installation which never bakes terrain
# should not have to carry:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.terrain-worker.yml up -d
#
# REQUIRED with that overlay, which refuses to start without it — it is what tells the application
# there is a worker to hand its bakes to. Set with no worker running, a build waits for a bake
# that nothing will ever do.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__BakeEnabled=true
# Memory one bake may use. The tile-maker takes its heap as a percentage of this and has no other
# setting for it, so this is the only knob there is — and short of memory it does not fail, it
# stops refining and quietly writes coarser tiles than were asked for. A couple of hundred square
# kilometres with one fine island in it was measured peaking around 2.5 GB.
# SILEXGIS_TERRAIN_WORKER_MEMORY=8g
# How often the worker looks for something to bake, in seconds.
# SILEXGIS_TERRAIN_WORKER_POLL_SECONDS=2
# Where the application leaves a request and the worker leaves its answer. Both sides must name
# the same directory and both compose files already do, so leave this alone: it is a path inside
# the api container, on the same volume the worker mounts.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__SpoolRoot=/data/terrain/spool
# How long a build waits for the worker to take its bake before deciding nothing is going to. This
# is what tells a worker nobody started apart from one that is still starting, so it is generous:
# a request waiting costs nothing, and a wrong answer here sends somebody looking for a crash that
# never happened. Values outside 5 seconds to an hour are brought back inside that range, so 0 is
# not "wait indefinitely": it becomes 5 seconds, and reports a starting worker as one that is absent.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__BakePickupSeconds=300
# How long one bake may run before it is abandoned. The queue a build runs on has no time limit of
# its own, so this is the only one there is; it is meant to catch a bake that has stopped making
# progress rather than to cap a large one, and half a day is far beyond anything measured. Values
# outside a minute to seven days are brought back inside that range, so 0 is not "no limit": it
# becomes a minute, and every real bake is then abandoned before it can finish.
# SILEXGIS__Terrain__BakeTimeoutSeconds=43200

# --- Data integrity -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How often a background pass re-checks the map data for internal inconsistencies. Findings
# are logged and shown in the admin jobs list; nothing is changed automatically. 00:00:00
# turns the schedule off, leaving the pass available to start by hand. A day is written
# d.hh:mm:ss, so one day is 1.00:00:00 — "24:00:00" would be read as twenty-four days.
# SILEXGIS__FeatureIntegrity__Interval=1.00:00:00

# --- Overdue parties ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How often a background pass checks whether a party that said when it would be back is late,
# and tells the people the trip names when it is. The interval is also how late that message
# can be, so it is minutes rather than hours. 00:00:00 turns the check off entirely, for an
# installation whose members do not use it — nobody is then told, whatever a trip has recorded.
# Anything above 00:30:00 is treated as 00:30:00: a trip showing an armed check reports a check
# that has not run for an hour as unchecked, and a warning every armed trip carries permanently
# is a warning nobody reads.
# SILEXGIS__TripCallout__SweepInterval=00:15:00
#
# How far ahead of a trip, or of a club event, the people it concerns are reminded that it is
# coming up. The same pass sends both, so that something put back or called off stops reminding
# anybody about a date that is no longer true. One setting for both, and not per reader. Written
# d.hh:mm:ss, so two days is 2.00:00:00. 00:00:00 sends no reminders and leaves the overdue check
# running.
# SILEXGIS__TripCallout__ReminderLead=2.00:00:00

# --- Uploads --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Largest file an upload may carry, in bytes. Default 536870912 (512 MB), which is sized for
# long scans and field recordings rather than photographs alone.
#
# Three limits govern one upload and they have to move together. The application's limit is
# this value; the request-body and multipart limits are derived from it automatically. The
# third is the reverse proxy in front, which refuses an oversized body before the application
# ever sees it — the bundled web service allows 1 GB, so any value up to that works as is.
# Above 1 GB, edit client/nginx.conf and rebuild the web image: that value is compiled into
# the image and cannot be set from here.
# SILEXGIS_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=536870912

# How much stored content one account may own, in bytes. 0 (the default) means no personal
# limit, which is what a club installation wants: the people using it are known to each other,
# and a quota that has to be raised by hand the first time somebody scans a survey is a support
# request rather than a protection. An individual account can be given its own figure, which
# overrides this one.
# SILEXGIS_USER_QUOTA_BYTES=0

# How much the whole installation may hold, in bytes. 0 (the default) means no limit. This is
# the one that actually protects the disk — quotas are not allocations, nothing is reserved,
# and the sum of everybody's may legitimately exceed the volume. Whoever mounted the volume is
# the one who knows what belongs here.
# SILEXGIS_MAX_TOTAL_STORE_BYTES=0

# How many days a deleted document or photograph can still be got back before its bytes go.
# 0 is a real setting, for an installation where deleting means deleting.
# SILEXGIS_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Directories on the server's own disk an administrator may import from. Empty (the default)
# switches importing from the filesystem off entirely, and that is a security boundary rather
# than a capacity one: an import reads whatever the service account can open, so the reachable
# directories are named by whoever deploys the installation — not chosen by whoever is signed
# in. Under Docker the path is resolved inside the api container, so a root named here must
# also be mounted into it.
#
# One list, two features: it governs importing documents and reading elevation rasters for a terrain
# build alike. Deliberately one, because it answers one question — which directories on this machine
# the application may read — and two lists would be two places for an operator to get it wrong.
# SILEXGIS__Files__ImportRoots__0=/data/import

# First-run administrator (created only if the account does not exist yet).
SILEXGIS_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
SILEXGIS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-please

# Allow self-registration (default: false — admins create accounts).
SILEXGIS_OPEN_REGISTRATION=false

# Permission groups (comma-separated slugs, e.g. editors) that every new account joins —
# at self-registration and when an external provider auto-creates an account. Empty means
# new accounts start with the built-ins only: browse what is visible to them, keep their
# own saved views, take part in trips. This line passes straight through to the API.
# SILEXGIS__Auth__DefaultPermissionGroups=

# How many sign-in, password-reset and token requests one IP address may make per minute
# before the rest are refused. Sixty is generous for a person and mean for a script. Raise
# it where a whole club reaches the server through one outbound address, which is what a
# shared connection or a mobile carrier looks like from here — every user then counts
# against the same allowance. This line passes straight through to the API.
# SILEXGIS__Auth__RateLimitPerMinute=60

# Requests per minute per address to the printed-cave-code landing address (/q/<code>). Its
# own window rather than the sign-in one, so that a group standing at a cave entrance
# scanning labels through a single connection cannot spend everyone else's sign-in
# allowance. NOTE THIS IS ABUSE AND COST CONTROL, NOT A CONFIDENTIALITY CONTROL: a printed
# code is short and reproducible outside this server, so its whole space is exhaustible at
# any rate a person would tolerate. What makes that pointless is that a code which resolves
# discloses only this installation's own name — nothing that ties it to a cave, a place, a
# position or a person. This line passes straight through to the API.
# SILEXGIS__Qr__RateLimitPerMinute=60

# How long the SpeleoLoc mobile app may stay offline before a caver has to sign in on it
# again. The clock restarts at every successful sync rather than at sign-in, so a phone
# that syncs monthly never re-authenticates and one left in a drawer for seven weeks does.
# A lapsed credential costs a sign-in and never data: the phone's own database is what
# holds its work. This is deliberately longer than the browser's window, because the phone
# is the client that keeps a credential for weeks and the one that gets lost — which is
# also why changing or resetting an account's password signs every one of its devices out.
# Values outside 1 to 365 days are brought back inside that range, so a slipped digit
# cannot mint a credential lasting years. This line passes straight through to the API.
# SILEXGIS__Auth__SpeleoLocRefreshTokenDays=45

# --- Test installation (optional, NEVER on an installation people rely on) ------
# Seeds three demo accounts — a full administrator, an editor and a viewer — and prints
# their credentials on the sign-in page for anyone who reaches it. That disclosure is the
# feature: a throwaway test instance wants strangers trying it out, and nothing else does.
# Anyone on the internet then holds the administrator login, so the switch defaults off.
# Pair it with a scheduled data reset (see silexgis-reset in docs/DEPLOY-SERVER.md) so the
# instance returns to a known state whatever visitors do to it. All accounts share one
# password (default test-login-pass-1); a change here is applied at the next start, so the
# page never advertises a credential that does not work. Lines pass straight to the API.
# SILEXGIS__TestLogins__Enabled=true
# SILEXGIS__TestLogins__Password=test-login-pass-1

# --- Reviewed import limits (optional) -----------------------------------------
# How much of one uploaded survey or GPS file the review screen takes on. Both were sized
# against hand-recorded lists of entrances; what people actually upload is a GPS unit
# emptied at the end of a season, where a single day's track log is tens of thousands of
# points on its own.
#
# The most rows one review reads out of a file that has been imported. A larger file is
# still imported whole and still drawn as a layer — only the candidate list is bounded, and
# the screen says so rather than showing a shortened list that reads exactly like a complete
# one. The list is paged to the browser, so this buys reviewable rows rather than rows drawn
# at once; the cost is the single scan that ranks and de-duplicates them.
# SILEXGIS__Import__MaxScanRows=150000
# The most objects one confirmation creates, deliberately equal to the review limit above so
# that a list you can see is a list you can confirm in one act. Confirmation runs as a
# background job rather than inside the request, so a large one costs a job that takes a
# while, not one that cannot finish. Lower it if you would rather your undo units stayed
# small: one confirmation is one transaction and reverts as a single thing.
# SILEXGIS__Import__MaxCommitItems=150000
# These lines pass straight through to the API container.

# --- Mobile sync limits (optional) ---------------------------------------------
# What a phone is told it may expect before it starts talking. It asks once, then sizes
# its own pages and batches to the answer, which is why these are announced rather than
# discovered by a request that fails in a cave with no signal. Raising them costs memory
# and response time per request; lowering them costs round trips. The shipped figures suit
# a mobile connection rather than any storage limit. Values outside 1 to 5000 are brought
# back inside that range. These lines pass straight through to the API container.
# SILEXGIS__Sync__PageSizeMax=500
# SILEXGIS__Sync__UploadRowsMax=500
# The page a phone gets when it asks for one without naming a size, deliberately well
# under the ceiling above: a first sync happens on whatever connection a cave's car park
# has, and a page that has to be retried whole is cheaper to retry small. Set above the
# ceiling it is served as the ceiling rather than refused — the request it applies to
# named no size to argue with — and below 1 it is brought back to 1.
# SILEXGIS__Sync__DefaultPageSize=100
# Whether a full administrator may read a sync set belonging to somebody else. Off, so
# that an installation allows it deliberately: a sync set names, in the clear, which caves
# a particular caver's phone carries and which club its rows are created for, and that is a
# statement about where that person goes rather than about the registry. Turn it on where
# your members expect an administrator to be able to answer "which caves does this phone
# hold?". It widens reading one set whose identifier the administrator already knows and
# nothing else: the listing still answers with the caller's own sets, so it can never be
# used to count anybody else's; replacing and deleting a set stay the owner's alone; and no
# phone can transfer through a set it does not own, whatever account it is signed in as.
# Note what that means in practice: the identifier has to come from the caver, from the
# phone that holds it, or from the database. Nothing in the web interface shows another
# account's set identifier, and this setting deliberately does not add a way to find one —
# it answers "what does this set carry?" for somebody who was handed the identifier, never
# "which sets does that caver have?".
# SILEXGIS__Sync__AllowAdministratorRead=false
# How near an existing feature has to be to a row a phone just created for the answer to mention it.
# Reporting only; 0 turns it off, and the ceiling is 5000.
# SILEXGIS__Sync__DuplicateRadiusMeters=50
#
# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO SALT SETTING HERE. The printed-code derivation has a salt, and it is a
# compatibility constant rather than a secret: it is fixed precisely so that two datasets which
# have never been in contact derive the same printed code from the same place code, which is what
# lets a label printed by one of them be recognised by the other. Changing it would harden
# nothing and would silently decide that every code already printed on a cave wall no longer
# matches anything derived afterwards. Nothing on the server derives a code in any case — a
# scanned label is answered by looking the stored value up, never by recomputing it — so the salt
# is compiled in beside the derivation it belongs to and is not an installation's choice. If a
# server-side derivation is ever added and has to agree with a differently-built device, that is
# the change that introduces a setting, together with recomputing every code that must keep
# agreeing.

# --- External login providers (optional; none configured by default) ---------------
# Federate Google / GitHub / any OIDC issuer into local accounts. Add one block per
# provider, numbered from 0. Register the redirect URI shown below with the provider.
# These SILEXGIS__* lines pass straight through to the API container.
#
# Register this exact redirect URI with the provider (name = the __0__Name value):
#   <SILEXGIS_PUBLIC_URL>/api/v1/signin-<name>
#
# Google (OIDC; issuer is built in):
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__0__Name=google
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__0__Type=google
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__0__DisplayName=Google
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__0__ClientId=your-google-client-id
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__0__ClientSecret=your-google-client-secret
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__0__AllowCreate=false
#
# GitHub (OAuth 2.0):
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__1__Name=github
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__1__Type=github
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__1__DisplayName=GitHub
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__1__ClientId=your-github-client-id
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__1__ClientSecret=your-github-client-secret
#
# Generic OIDC (e.g. Keycloak, Authentik):
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__2__Name=sso
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__2__Type=oidc
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__2__DisplayName=Company SSO
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__2__Authority=https://sso.example.com/realms/main
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__2__ClientId=silexgis
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalProviders__2__ClientSecret=your-client-secret
#
# AllowCreate=true provisions a local account on first sign-in (needs a provider-verified
# email); default false requires the account to exist or be linked from account settings.
# Hide the password form entirely once providers are set:
# SILEXGIS__Auth__ExternalOnly=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email delivery (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# With no mail server configured everything still works: confirmation links and
# sign-in codes are written to the API container log instead of being sent.
# These can also be set from the admin UI (Messaging), which overrides what is here.
#
# SILEXGIS__Mail__Enabled=true
# SILEXGIS__Mail__Host=smtp.example.org
# SILEXGIS__Mail__Port=587
# Security: Auto (default) | StartTls | SslOnConnect (port 465) | None
# SILEXGIS__Mail__Security=Auto
# SILEXGIS__Mail__Username=silexgis@example.org
# SILEXGIS__Mail__Password=your-smtp-password
# SILEXGIS__Mail__FromAddress=silexgis@example.org
# SILEXGIS__Mail__FromName=Cave Register
# SILEXGIS__Mail__ReplyTo=
# Only for an internal relay with a self-signed certificate — it removes the
# protection on the password above:
# SILEXGIS__Mail__AcceptInvalidCertificate=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMS delivery (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any gateway that accepts an HTTP request works. {to}, {text} and {from} are
# substituted into the URL and body, escaped for the content type.
#
# SILEXGIS__Sms__Enabled=true
# SILEXGIS__Sms__Url=https://gateway.example.org/send
# SILEXGIS__Sms__Method=POST
# SILEXGIS__Sms__ContentType=application/x-www-form-urlencoded
# SILEXGIS__Sms__BodyTemplate=To={to}&From={from}&Body={text}
# Whole Authorization header value, e.g. "Basic <base64 of user:pass>" or "Bearer <token>":
# SILEXGIS__Sms__AuthHeader=Basic eW91cjpjcmVkcw==
# SILEXGIS__Sms__From=+40712345678
#
# Twilio, as a worked example:
# SILEXGIS__Sms__Url=https://api.twilio.com/2010-04-01/Accounts/<ACCOUNT_SID>/Messages.json
# SILEXGIS__Sms__AuthHeader=Basic <base64 of ACCOUNT_SID:AUTH_TOKEN>
# SILEXGIS__Sms__BodyTemplate=To={to}&From={from}&Body={text}
#
# A JSON gateway instead:
# SILEXGIS__Sms__ContentType=application/json
# SILEXGIS__Sms__BodyTemplate={"to":"{to}","from":"{from}","message":"{text}"}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign-in policy (optional; also editable in the admin UI)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Refuse sign-in until the address is confirmed. Has no effect while no mail
# server is configured — otherwise nobody could ever confirm.
# SILEXGIS__Security__RequireConfirmedEmail=false
# SILEXGIS__Security__SendConfirmationOnRegistration=true
# Which second factors accounts may use:
# SILEXGIS__Security__AuthenticatorTwoFactorEnabled=true
# SILEXGIS__Security__EmailTwoFactorEnabled=true
# Texted codes are off by default: a number can be moved to another SIM by
# persuading a mobile operator, which makes SMS the weakest of the three.
# SILEXGIS__Security__SmsTwoFactorEnabled=false
# SILEXGIS__Security__TwoFactorCodeLifetimeMinutes=5
# SILEXGIS__Security__TwoFactorResendIntervalSeconds=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Location protection (optional; also editable in the admin UI)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Whether someone who may not see a protected cave's exact position is still
# told which documents are attached to it. The documents themselves are served
# either way — this only affects the pairing. Off by default: naming the cave a
# report is about is a much smaller disclosure than where the cave is, but it is
# still one. Switching it on never reveals a position, and a photo carrying its
# own capture point stays unpaired with a protected cave whatever this says.
# SILEXGIS__Protection__RevealProtectedAssociations=false

# Name used in the messages this installation sends ({appName} in the templates):
# SILEXGIS__About__InstanceName=SilexGIS

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Document conversion (optional; needs the converter overlay running)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OpenDocument files have no pages until something
# lays them out, so they cannot be shown page by page in the browser the way a
# PDF can. Start the converter overlay and set these two, and each such upload
# gets a PDF copy stored beside it — the original is never touched — which the
# application then draws pages from. Leave them out and those files are stored,
# searched and downloaded exactly as before, with no page to look at.
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.convert.yml up -d
# SILEXGIS__Conversion__Enabled=true
# SILEXGIS__Conversion__Url=http://convert:3000
# How long one conversion may take. Long documents are slow; a request cut
# short would be recorded as a document that would not open.
# SILEXGIS__Conversion__TimeoutSeconds=120

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The coordinate system distances are measured in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything is stored in WGS 84, whose units are degrees — right for storing,
# useless for measuring, since a degree of longitude is a different distance at
# every latitude. Questions asked in metres in three dimensions (how deep a
# passage lies under the surface, how close two caves come to each other) are
# computed in a projected system instead, and this is which one.
#
# The default is UTM zone 35 north on WGS 84, covering the eastern Carpathians.
# An installation elsewhere sets its own zone: 32634 for western Romania, 25832
# for central Europe on ETRS89, and so on. The value is checked when the
# application starts, so a code that cannot be resolved stops the container with
# a message naming this setting rather than producing wrong distances later.
#
# A national grid is usually the wrong choice here even where one exists: those
# are often defined on a different datum, and the datum shift makes the
# horizontal result depend on the altitude supplied with the point — so the same
# passage lands in slightly different places depending on whether its depth was
# included. Fine for drawing a map, wrong for a measurement this application
# then compares against another measurement.
# SILEXGIS__Spatial__WorkingSrid=32635
# Announcements to a caving group (optional; also editable in the admin UI)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What an announcement to a whole caving group may cost this installation. Off means off:
# while it is off, a channel that charges for every message cannot be chosen by anybody,
# resolves to nothing when a preference for it is read, and no outbound copy on it is ever
# created — it is an absent option rather than a hidden one. The charging channel is the text
# message, so switching this on can put real messages on a real bill: it additionally needs
# SILEXGIS_SMS_* below (or the admin SMS page) pointing at a working gateway, and only an
# announcement to a caving group may use it. A member is texted only if they have confirmed a
# telephone number on their own security page. The cap bounds what a day's announcements may
# cost. Both are also editable from the admin pages, and what is saved there replaces what is
# set here.
# SILEXGIS_ANNOUNCEMENTS_PAID_CHANNELS_ENABLED=false
# SILEXGIS_ANNOUNCEMENTS_DAILY_PAID_MESSAGE_CAP=100

# How many members an announcement is written to inside the request that sends it. A larger
# roster is recorded once and handed out by a background pass instead.
# SILEXGIS_NOTIFICATIONS_ANNOUNCEMENT_FAN_OUT_LIMIT=50

# The Romanian national cave catalogue (speologie.org) — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading speologie.org, and importing caves from it, needs a personal API key. Get one from
# "Editează profilul" on a speologie.org account, or ask their administrators. Leave this unset
# and the feature is simply absent: the screens are not offered in the rail, the endpoints say
# so plainly, and nothing here ever reaches the network. That is a supported installation, not
# a broken one — most installations of this application have nothing to do with the Romanian
# register.
#
# THE KEY IS A SECRET AND BELONGS HERE, NOT IN A COMMITTED FILE. It identifies a person on
# somebody else's service.
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__ApiKey=sk_your_key_here

# Where the catalogue answers. The shipped value is correct; it is configurable so a test can
# point elsewhere. Note the www: the bare domain answers every request with a redirect to it,
# so pointing at speologie.org doubles every call for nothing.
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__Endpoint=https://www.speologie.org/api/graphql

# How politely this installation asks. The catalogue is a small volunteer-run service whose own
# documentation asks not to be hammered, and this application has nothing to gain from being
# fast against it — a person searching for a cave waits a second either way. Calls are made one
# at a time for the whole process; this is the minimum gap between them, in milliseconds.
# Lowering it is a decision about somebody else's capacity, not about this installation's speed.
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__MinRequestIntervalMs=1000
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__TimeoutSeconds=20
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__MaxRetries=2

# The largest page of search results asked for. The catalogue's own ceiling is 100 and it
# clamps silently rather than refusing, so this is also enforced before asking.
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__MaxPageSize=50

# How many caves one confirmation may import or refresh. Small on purpose: the wizard is for
# choosing caves, and somebody selecting two hundred by hand has stopped reviewing them.
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__MaxSelection=100

# How much of a catalogue description is kept. Theirs are usually a few hundred characters and
# occasionally over a megabyte of markup pasted out of a word processor; what is cut is marked
# as cut, and the cave keeps a link to the page it came from.
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__MaxDescriptionChars=20000

# What this installation calls itself when it asks, so the far end can tell an application's
# traffic from a scraper's and reach somebody if it needs to.
# SILEXGIS__Speologie__UserAgent=SilexGIS

# --- Grottocenter: looking a cave up in the international community database ---------------
# Optional, and OFF unless the line below is uncommented. When it is off nothing is offered on
# any screen, the endpoint says plainly that this installation does not do it, and no request
# leaves the machine. That is a supported installation, not a broken one.
#
# What turning it on means, stated plainly because it is the one integration that sends
# something outward on a signed-in person's action: pressing the lookup button on a cave sends
# THAT CAVE'S NAME to grottocenter.org. It sends nothing else — never a position, never a
# description — and only somebody who may edit the cave can cause it. What comes back is shown
# to that person, and only the identifier they accept is stored; nothing else is kept, so
# nothing here can go stale while still looking authoritative.
# SILEXGIS__Grottocenter__Enabled=true

# Where the database answers, and where a person is sent to read an entry. The shipped values
# are correct; they are configurable so a test can point elsewhere.
# SILEXGIS__Grottocenter__Endpoint=https://api.grottocenter.org/api/v1
# SILEXGIS__Grottocenter__EntryUrlTemplate=https://www.grottocenter.org/ui/entry/{0}

# How politely this installation asks. Calls are made one at a time for the whole process; this
# is the minimum gap between them, in milliseconds. Lowering it is a decision about somebody
# else's capacity, not about this installation's speed.
# SILEXGIS__Grottocenter__MinRequestIntervalMs=1000
# SILEXGIS__Grottocenter__TimeoutSeconds=15

# How many candidates one lookup offers. A person picking from a long list has stopped reading it.
# SILEXGIS__Grottocenter__MaxCandidates=10

# What this installation calls itself when it asks, so the far end can tell an application's
# traffic from a scraper's and reach somebody if it needs to.
# SILEXGIS__Grottocenter__UserAgent=SilexGIS
# --- Photo libraries: stopping one without a redeploy ------------------------------------
# Which photo libraries this installation has is decided here: the overlay is in COMPOSE_FILE and
# an address and a credential are set below, or the library does not exist as far as the
# application is concerned. Nothing on any screen changes that.
#
# Stopping one is different and does not need this file. An administrator can suspend a configured
# library from the installation settings, per library, while everything is running: the map layer,
# the browsing page, the search and the pictures all stop, no request goes to it at all, and the
# setting survives a restart. It is the answer to a library misbehaving, a credential about to be
# replaced, or an afternoon's work on the machine it runs on — and it is reversible from the same
# screen. What it cannot do is connect a library: the pages can stop something that exists and
# never start something that does not.
#
# Suspending changes nothing on the library's side. It goes on running, indexing and serving
# whoever signs into it directly. To stop the library itself, stop its containers —
# `docker compose stop immich-server` or `docker compose stop photoprism` — and to stop paying for
# four idle containers, drop the overlay from COMPOSE_FILE.

# --- Photo library: Immich (only when using docker-compose.immich.yml) -------------------
# The other optional photo library, independent of PhotoPrism in every respect: its own database,
# its own storage, either or both or neither.
#
# PIN THE BUILD. Immich breaks its own API at every major and does not backport fixes or support
# downgrades, so a moving tag turns a routine pull into an unplanned migration.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_VERSION=v3.1.0
#
# REQUIRED with that overlay: the password for its own database container.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
#
# WHERE THE PHOTOGRAPHS ARE, bound READ-ONLY, containing a `.silexgis-photo-library` marker file.
# Immich's external-library code deletes files on a writable mount, so read-only is not a
# preference; the marker is what stops the container starting against a disk that failed to mount.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_LIBRARY_DIR=/srv/photos
#
# Loopback host port for its own web interface.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_BIND=127.0.0.1
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_PORT=2283
#
# True for exactly one start, so the first administrator account can be created, then false.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_ALLOW_SETUP=false
#
# RECORD THE ADMINISTRATOR YOU CREATE. Immich keeps no other copy, and recovering it means
# `docker exec -it <immich server container> /usr/src/app/server/bin/immich-admin
# reset-admin-password` — which works, but is a detour nobody should need twice. The tool is not
# on PATH, which is why it looks absent.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.org
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
#
# The API key the application reads this library with. Mint it in the library's own account
# settings and give it EXACTLY these three permissions, never `all`:
#   map.read     the positions the map layer draws
#   asset.view   the pictures, which this application proxies rather than linking
#   asset.read   the browse list and the search
# A key carrying only the first two passes the health check and then refuses every listing and
# every search, so the health surface names any of the three that is missing.
# SILEXGIS__PhotoLibraries__Immich__Enabled=true
# SILEXGIS__PhotoLibraries__Immich__BaseUrl=http://immich-server:2283
# SILEXGIS__PhotoLibraries__Immich__ApiKey=

# --- Photo library: PhotoPrism (only when using docker-compose.photoprism.yml) ------------
# An optional, self-contained photo library with its own database and its own storage. It
# shares nothing with the application's own photo storage, and nothing with Immich. Adding
# the overlay is what switches it on; without it, none of this is read.
#
# PIN THE BUILD. PhotoPrism has no semantic versioning and no deprecation policy: its tags are
# dates, and `latest` moves. A moving tag turns a routine `docker compose pull` into an
# unplanned upgrade of a third-party stack. Confirm a tag is published before pinning it:
#   curl -s "https://hub.docker.com/v2/repositories/photoprism/photoprism/tags?page_size=10"
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_VERSION=260728
#
# REQUIRED with that overlay. The password this image ships is the word "insecure", so it gets
# its own login rather than a shipped default.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_ADMIN_USER=admin
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
#
# The address a browser reaches it on, trailing slash included. Wrong here and every link the
# product generates points somewhere the browser cannot follow.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_SITE_URL=http://localhost:2342/
#
# Loopback host port. Docker publishes ports ahead of this host's firewall rules, so binding
# to 0.0.0.0 really is reachable from outside even when the firewall says otherwise.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_BIND=127.0.0.1
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_PORT=2342
#
# WHERE THE PHOTOGRAPHS ARE. In the shipped mode this is a directory the operator's disks are
# mounted under, bound READ-ONLY, and it must contain a file named `.silexgis-photo-library`.
# That marker is not decoration: it is what makes a disk that failed to mount stop the
# container instead of presenting an empty directory that Docker invented — after which every
# photograph resolves to nothing and a single thumbnail request deletes it from the index.
# On Windows write it with forward slashes, e.g. D:/Photos.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_LIBRARY_DIR=/srv/photos
#
# Must stay true in the shipped read-only mode; set false only when switching the overlay to
# import photographs into the product's own storage instead.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_READONLY=true
#
# Turns off the product's own interactive world map and its reverse geocoding, both of which
# transmit the library's spatial distribution to third parties. Measured against build 260728:
# this does NOT disable the geographic API, so photograph positions still reach the
# application. Re-check after a version change.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_DISABLE_PLACES=true
#
# Indexing workers. Never "auto": auto takes half the visible CPUs, so on a large host a first
# index run helps itself to half the machine and starves whatever it was installed beside.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_WORKERS=4
#
# Leave both blank and no picture leaves this machine. Naming a vision service here sends
# image content to it.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_VISION_URI=
# SILEXGIS_PHOTOPRISM_VISION_KEY=

# --- Photo library: Immich (only when using docker-compose.immich.yml) --------------------
# A second, independent photo library, with its own PostgreSQL and its own Valkey. It shares
# nothing with this installation's database, and it must never be pointed at it: Immich expects
# superuser on its cluster, sets instance-wide startup options, and its own restore procedure
# begins by deleting the data directory.
#
# PIN THE RELEASE, on the v3 line. `release` moves under you at update time, and a v2 tag does
# not start against the shapes this overlay uses.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_VERSION=v3.1.0
#
# Its own cluster, its own password. REQUIRED with that overlay.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
#
# WHERE THE PHOTOGRAPHS ARE, in the shipped read-only mode. The directory must contain a file
# named `.silexgis-photo-library`. That marker is the difference between a disk that failed to
# mount stopping the container and Immich scanning an empty directory, concluding every asset is
# gone, and trashing the whole library — after which every album, named face and edit is lost
# once the retention window passes. The photographs survive on the unmounted disk; the work
# Immich did does not. On Windows use forward slashes, e.g. D:/Photos.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_LIBRARY_DIR=/srv/photos
#
# Loopback host port. Published ports bypass this host's firewall rules.
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_BIND=127.0.0.1
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_PORT=2283
#
# FIRST-RUN SIGN-UP. Immich makes the first account that registers the administrator, and the
# route stays open while this is true — so an instance that is started but not yet claimed is
# claimed by whoever reaches it first. Set it true for exactly ONE start, create the account,
# then set it false and recreate the container. Confirmed against v3.1.0: with it false the
# claim route answers "Admin setup is disabled".
# SILEXGIS_IMMICH_ALLOW_SETUP=false
#
# The zone the library reads timestamps in. Set rather than mounted, so it works on Windows too.
# SILEXGIS_PHOTO_LIBRARY_TZ=UTC
